The important distinction is that the LAW specifies general powers of the Board, restrictions based on Producer Companies, and contributions allowed for national defence [1][2][3][4]. Meanwhile, the COMPANY POSITION states that the Companies Act, 2013, and applicable rules and regulations made under it, govern the company's current corporate matters, including board authority [5]. Under the LAW, the LAW says the Board of Directors of a company shall be entitled to exercise all such powers, and to do all such acts and things, as the company is authorised to exercise and do [1]. In exercising such power, the Board shall be subject to the provisions contained in that behalf in the Act, or in the memorandum or articles, or in any regulations duly made thereunder [1]. The LAW says the Board shall not exercise any power which is directed or required to be exercised by the company in general meeting [1]. No regulation made by the company in general meeting shall invalidate any prior act of the Board which would have been valid if that regulation had not been made [1]. The LAW also says for a Producer Company, all specified board powers shall be exercised by means of resolution passed at its meeting on behalf of the Producer Company [2]. The LAW states that a director or a group of directors, who do not constitute the Board, shall not exercise any of the powers exercisable by it [2]. For a Producer Company, the LAW specifies that the Board shall exercise certain powers only by means of resolutions passed at the annual general meeting of its Members, namely:
- approval of budget and adoption of annual accounts ;
- approval of patronage bonus ;
- issue of bonus shares ;
- declaration of limited return and decision on the distribution of patronage ;
- specify the conditions and limits of loans that may be given by the Board to any director ; and
- approval of any transaction reserved in the articles for approval by Members [4][4][4][4][4][4]. The LAW further states that the Board of Directors may, notwithstanding anything contained in sections 180, 181 and 182 or any other provision of the Act, contribute such amount as it thinks fit to the National Defence Fund or any other Fund approved by the Central Government for the purpose of national defence [3]. Every company shall disclose the total contributed amounts in its profits and loss account during the financial year [3].

Sources
[1] Companies Act, 2013, s. 179
[2] Companies Act, 2013, s. 378R
[3] Companies Act, 2013, s. 183
[4] Companies Act, 2013, s. 378S
[5] Legal Constitution L1.10 §4.1

